     1	# Current status and comparable baseline
     2	
     3	Snapshot: **2026-10-14 (PT)**. Book: *10000 Selected Problems in Sciences: Physics*, Science Press (2009), pp. 333–334; author Zhao Daomu. The provided source is preserved verbatim in `source/problem.zh.txt`.
     4	
     5	## Selection correction: the chapter is not a verified easy open theorem
     6	
     7	The original book goal remains **OPEN-BLOCKED in this project**: we have not supplied a necessary-and-sufficient classification for all source models and physical propagation environments. This is **not** a claim that no such classification exists anywhere in the literature. The 2009 chapter itself explicitly calls its questions unresolved; we did **not** locate a current primary source explicitly designating that unrestricted chapter-level question as a still-open theorem in 2026. Search misses do not establish unsolved status.
     8	
     9	The broad question “are there more general invariance conditions?” has received affirmative answers, including publications before the book and much broader structured-source examples afterward. Accordingly, the initial selection as a problem both “still unsolved” and “not too hard” is **not validated**. The tractable obligation here is the finite-mode **all-scalar-probes** classification, reducible to affine Bloch-sphere geometry; that does not make the unrestricted Fresnel/Maxwell classification easy. The simulator is not an argument about difficulty. This folder is a partial research contribution and a correction to the selection, not a substitute solved problem.
    10	
    11	## Observables and quantifiers must be distinguished
    12	
    13	1. **Spectrum:** the local normalized spectral density, determined by `tr J`; it may be invariant over a far-zone angular plane without being equal to every source-plane local spectrum.
    14	2. **Degree of polarization:** the eigenvalue contrast of the local ensemble coherency matrix `J`, at a specified frequency. Constant degree does not require a constant ellipse/orientation.
    15	3. **Pattern invariance:** `P(r,z)=P(r,0)` can allow a nonuniform transverse pattern. It is weaker/different from one common degree at every observation point.
    16	4. **Source to far zone**, **all Fresnel distances**, **all scalar linear probes**, **every random mixture**, and **full Maxwell propagation** are different quantified statements.
    17	5. Averaging instantaneous degrees is not the degree of an ensemble-averaged coherency matrix. A full-plane integrated Stokes vector is also a different observable.
    18	
    19	## Primary-source theorem/evidence map
    20	
    21	This is a scoped, verified map, **not an assertion that every theorem in statistical optics has been exhaustively reviewed**. Full-text access, abstract-only evidence and metadata-only leads are separated rather than fabricating hypotheses from titles.
    22	
    23	### Scalar spectral propagation and its electromagnetic extension
    24	
    25	- **Emil Wolf**, “Invariance of the Spectrum of Light on Propagation,” *Physical Review Letters* **56**, 1370–1372 (1986), DOI [10.1103/PhysRevLett.56.1370](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.56.1370). Publisher/Crossref metadata verified; APS full text returned HTTP 403. The book attributes the scaling law to this paper. Its statement must not be promoted to an unrestricted full-Maxwell necessary condition: the source class, observation region and normalized-spectrum definition matter. The independently written Fourier proof in `RESULTS.md` C1 supplies all hypotheses for the precise formulation used here, without relying on inaccessible details of this paper.
    26	- **Emil Wolf**, “Non-cosmological redshifts of spectral lines,” *Nature* **326**, 363–365 (1987), DOI [10.1038/326363a0](https://doi.org/10.1038/326363a0). Metadata verified. A correlation-induced redistribution of spectral weight is not a change in individual photon frequency or a demonstrated explanation of cosmological redshifts. The book's astrophysical wording is not adopted as a conclusion here.
    27	- **Greg Gbur, Taco D. Visser and Emil Wolf**, “Anomalous behavior of spectra near phase singularities of focused waves,” *Physical Review Letters* **88**, 013901 (2002), [10.1103/PhysRevLett.88.013901](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.88.013901). Book-cited reference; APS full-text access failed. This is an example of spectral noninvariance near focused-field singularities, not a universal classification.
    28	- **Jixiong Pu, Olga Korotkova and Emil Wolf**, “Invariance and noninvariance of the spectra of stochastic electromagnetic beams on propogation” [publisher spelling], *Optics Letters* **31**, 2097–2099 (2006), DOI [10.1364/OL.31.002097](https://doi.org/10.1364/OL.31.002097). [Primary publisher abstract](https://opg.optica.org/services/open_md.cfm?uri=ol-31-14-2097) read. It concerns beams generated by **planar secondary stochastic electromagnetic sources**, and derives an electromagnetic scaling law which makes the normalized spectrum the same **throughout the far zone and across the source**. This electromagnetic extension was already published before the 2009 book. The abstract gives sufficiency, not an all-source/all-distance theorem. Full-text formula was not verified here.
    29	
    30	**Exact baseline used in this folder:** in the entire ideal paraxial angular plane, `I(s;k)=k² Ghat(ks;k)` for the midpoint-integrated trace correlation `G`. Spectral separability is equivalent to `G(v;k)=B(k)g(kv)` under Fourier-inversion assumptions; see C1. Only `G`, not every entry of the full source kernel, is constrained by spectral measurements. Physical bounded-angle and nonparaxial restrictions invalidate a naive unrestricted inverse-Fourier necessity.
    31	
    32	### Polarization in free space
    33	
