     3	Snapshot: **2026-10-14 (PT)**. Book: *10000 Selected Problems in Sciences: Physics*, Science Press (2009), pp. 333–334; author Zhao Daomu. The provided source is preserved verbatim in `source/problem.zh.txt`.
     4	
     5	## Selection correction: the chapter is not a verified easy open theorem
     6	
     7	The original book goal remains **OPEN-BLOCKED in this project**: we have not supplied a necessary-and-sufficient classification for all source models and physical propagation environments. This is **not** a claim that no such classification exists anywhere in the literature. The 2009 chapter itself explicitly calls its questions unresolved; we did **not** locate a current primary source explicitly designating that unrestricted chapter-level question as a still-open theorem in 2026. Search misses do not establish unsolved status.
     8	
     9	The broad question “are there more general invariance conditions?” has received affirmative answers, including publications before the book and much broader structured-source examples afterward. Accordingly, the initial selection as a problem both “still unsolved” and “not too hard” is **not validated**. The tractable obligation here is the finite-mode **all-scalar-probes** classification, reducible to affine Bloch-sphere geometry; that does not make the unrestricted Fresnel/Maxwell classification easy. The simulator is not an argument about difficulty. This folder is a partial research contribution and a correction to the selection, not a substitute solved problem.
    10	
    11	## Observables and quantifiers must be distinguished
    12	
    13	1. **Spectrum:** the local normalized spectral density, determined by `tr J`; it may be invariant over a far-zone angular plane without being equal to every source-plane local spectrum.
    14	2. **Degree of polarization:** the eigenvalue contrast of the local ensemble coherency matrix `J`, at a specified frequency. Constant degree does not require a constant ellipse/orientation.
    15	3. **Pattern invariance:** `P(r,z)=P(r,0)` can allow a nonuniform transverse pattern. It is weaker/different from one common degree at every observation point.
    16	4. **Source to far zone**, **all Fresnel distances**, **all scalar linear probes**, **every random mixture**, and **full Maxwell propagation** are different quantified statements.
    17	5. Averaging instantaneous degrees is not the degree of an ensemble-averaged coherency matrix. A full-plane integrated Stokes vector is also a different observable.
    18	
    19	## Primary-source theorem/evidence map
    20	
    21	This is a scoped, verified map, **not an assertion that every theorem in statistical optics has been exhaustively reviewed**. Full-text access, abstract-only evidence and metadata-only leads are separated rather than fabricating hypotheses from titles.
