    23	### Scalar spectral propagation and its electromagnetic extension
    24	
    25	- **Emil Wolf**, “Invariance of the Spectrum of Light on Propagation,” *Physical Review Letters* **56**, 1370–1372 (1986), DOI [10.1103/PhysRevLett.56.1370](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.56.1370). Publisher/Crossref metadata verified; APS full text returned HTTP 403. The book attributes the scaling law to this paper. Its statement must not be promoted to an unrestricted full-Maxwell necessary condition: the source class, observation region and normalized-spectrum definition matter. The independently written Fourier proof in `RESULTS.md` C1 supplies all hypotheses for the precise formulation used here, without relying on inaccessible details of this paper.
    26	- **Emil Wolf**, “Non-cosmological redshifts of spectral lines,” *Nature* **326**, 363–365 (1987), DOI [10.1038/326363a0](https://doi.org/10.1038/326363a0). Metadata verified. A correlation-induced redistribution of spectral weight is not a change in individual photon frequency or a demonstrated explanation of cosmological redshifts. The book's astrophysical wording is not adopted as a conclusion here.
    27	- **Greg Gbur, Taco D. Visser and Emil Wolf**, “Anomalous behavior of spectra near phase singularities of focused waves,” *Physical Review Letters* **88**, 013901 (2002), [10.1103/PhysRevLett.88.013901](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.88.013901). Book-cited reference; APS full-text access failed. This is an example of spectral noninvariance near focused-field singularities, not a universal classification.
    28	- **Jixiong Pu, Olga Korotkova and Emil Wolf**, “Invariance and noninvariance of the spectra of stochastic electromagnetic beams on propogation” [publisher spelling], *Optics Letters* **31**, 2097–2099 (2006), DOI [10.1364/OL.31.002097](https://doi.org/10.1364/OL.31.002097). [Primary publisher abstract](https://opg.optica.org/services/open_md.cfm?uri=ol-31-14-2097) read. It concerns beams generated by **planar secondary stochastic electromagnetic sources**, and derives an electromagnetic scaling law which makes the normalized spectrum the same **throughout the far zone and across the source**. This electromagnetic extension was already published before the 2009 book. The abstract gives sufficiency, not an all-source/all-distance theorem. Full-text formula was not verified here.
    29	
    30	**Exact baseline used in this folder:** in the entire ideal paraxial angular plane, `I(s;k)=k² Ghat(ks;k)` for the midpoint-integrated trace correlation `G`. Spectral separability is equivalent to `G(v;k)=B(k)g(kv)` under Fourier-inversion assumptions; see C1. Only `G`, not every entry of the full source kernel, is constrained by spectral measurements. Physical bounded-angle and nonparaxial restrictions invalidate a naive unrestricted inverse-Fourier necessity.
    31	
    32	### Polarization in free space
    33	
    34	- **Daniel F. V. James**, “Change of polarization of light beams on propagation in free space,” *JOSA A* **11**, 1641–1643 (1994), DOI [10.1364/JOSAA.11.001641](https://doi.org/10.1364/JOSAA.11.001641). Metadata verified; not a theorem that polarization always changes. It demonstrates that propagation-induced change is possible.
    35	- **Emil Wolf**, “Polarization invariance in beam propagation,” *Optics Letters* **32**, 3400–3401 (2007), DOI [10.1364/OL.32.003400](https://doi.org/10.1364/OL.32.003400). [Primary abstract read](https://opg.optica.org/ol/abstract.cfm?uri=ol-32-23-3400). Its precise scope is a **uniformly polarized stochastic electromagnetic source of a wide class**, equality of degree **in the source plane and throughout the far zone**. The abstract explicitly says **“we derive sufficiency conditions”**. The book's “must satisfy” formulation is therefore too strong as an account of this paper. The excerpt's equal `xx/yy` and zero `xy` correlation condition is recorded as the book's description, not as a verified general necessity. Full text requires access / returned a bot challenge; no claimed line-by-line reconstruction.
    36	- **Daomu Zhao and Emil Wolf**, “Light beams whose degree of polarization does not change on propagation,” *Optics Communications* **281**, 3067–3070 (2008), DOI [10.1016/j.optcom.2008.02.006](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.optcom.2008.02.006). Title, authors, venue and pages are verified by Crossref and [Wolf's institutional publication list](https://www.sas.rochester.edu/pas/people/faculty/wolf_emil/index.html). This is a directly relevant follow-up by the book author, predating the book. The quasi-homogeneous characterization was located only in secondary search summaries; full text was inaccessible, so we do not assert its exact assumptions or theorem strength.
    37	- **Rosario Martínez-Herrero and Pedro M. Mejías**, “Electromagnetic fields that remain totally polarized under propagation,” *Optics Communications* **279**, 20–22 (2007), DOI [10.1016/j.optcom.2007.07.002](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.optcom.2007.07.002). Verified metadata; a relevant total-polarization boundary case, not taken as a universal partial-polarization theorem without its text.
    38	- **Xinyue Du and Daomu Zhao**, “Criterion for keeping completely unpolarized or completely polarized stochastic electromagnetic Gaussian Schell-model beams on propagation,” *Optics Express* **16**, 16172–16180 (2008), DOI [10.1364/OE.16.016172](https://doi.org/10.1364/OE.16.016172). Metadata verified; exact criterion not reconstructed from the title.
    39	- **XinHui Zhao, Yong Yao, Yunxu Sun and Chao Liu**, “Condition for Gaussian Schell-model beam to maintain the state of polarization on the propagation in free space,” *Optics Express* **17**, 17888–17894 (2009), DOI [10.1364/OE.17.017888](https://doi.org/10.1364/OE.17.017888). [Primary abstract read](https://opg.optica.org/services/open_md.cfm?uri=oe-17-20-17888). It explicitly supplies a **sufficient**, not necessary, condition: `delta_xx=delta_yy=delta_xy` and `sigma_x=sigma_y`. Under these assumptions a realizable GSM kernel factors as `W=g C`, so both the degree and the polarization state are preserved by polarization-independent scalar propagation. The off-diagonal constant `C_xy` need **not** vanish.
    40	- **Olga Korotkova**, “Sufficient condition for polarization invariance of beams generated by quasi-homogeneous sources,” *Optics Letters* **36**, 3768–3770 (2011), DOI [10.1364/OL.36.003768](https://doi.org/10.1364/OL.36.003768). [Primary abstract read](https://opg.optica.org/services/open_md.cfm?uri=ol-36-19-3768). The source class is **electromagnetic quasi-homogeneous**, the observation domain is **far zone**, and the result concerns both degree and state, with a Bessel-correlated example. The abstract does not expose its exact formula; it is not represented here as a general necessity theorem.
    41	
    42	### A verified non-Schell, all-distance patterned class (2022)
    43	
    44	**Rosario Martínez-Herrero, Gemma Piquero, Massimo Santarsiero, Franco Gori and Juan Carlos González de Sande**, “A class of vectorial pseudo-Schell model sources with structured coherence and polarization,” *Optics & Laser Technology* **152**, 108079 (2022), DOI [10.1016/j.optlastec.2022.108079](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.optlastec.2022.108079). [Institutional record](https://oa.upm.es/87042/); [full primary article read](https://oa.upm.es/87042/1/9906481.pdf).
    45	
    46	In polar coordinates, Eqs. (19)–(21) use a realizable radial kernel `W_PS(r1,r2)` times an angular polarization matrix. In this folder's conjugation convention its entries are proportional to
    47	
    48	\[
    49	\begin{pmatrix}
    50	\cos\theta_1\cos\theta_2 & b\cos\theta_1\cos(\theta_2-\alpha)\\
    51	b^*\cos(\theta_1-\alpha)\cos\theta_2 & \cos(\theta_1-\alpha)\cos(\theta_2-\alpha)
    52	\end{pmatrix},\quad |b|\leq1.
    53	\]
    54	
    55	The displayed Gaussian example has radial envelope proportional to `r exp(-r²/w0²)` and radial coherence `exp[-(r2-r1)²/delta²]`. Both angular functions involve the same absolute azimuthal order. Under the **paraxial free-space Fresnel integral**, Eq. (33) retains precisely the angular matrix and changes only the radial scalar kernel. Thus the normalized coherency matrix, polarization pattern, and degree are independent of `z` wherever the intensity is nonzero, although the degree is generally **not uniform in angle**. The on-axis dark point is excluded. This is an explicit, physically relevant answer to the book's request for more general conditions, not just a GSM example. It also warns against replacing invariance along propagation by invariance under every conceivable probe.
    56	
    57	### Deterministic ABCD and scalar versus vector environments
    58	
    59	The elementary factorization result `W=g C -> J=h C` holds at **all distances** for any common scalar kernel, including polarization-independent paraxial ABCD/Collins systems, apertures, Fresnel propagation and scalar angular-spectrum propagation. It is a direct linearity consequence, independently proved in C3; it is not advertised as new.
    60	
    61	For a polarization-dependent kernel `K_ij`, the common scalar factor cannot be pulled outside in the same way. Likewise, a full three-component Maxwell field has longitudinal components constrained by transversality, and the two-component DOP formula is no longer an unqualified full-field observable. This folder does not pretend that scalar angular spectrum is a full nonparaxial electromagnetic solution.
    62	
    63	### Random media and recent results
    64	
    65	- **Takashi Shirai and Emil Wolf**, “Coherence and polarization of electromagnetic beams modulated by random phase screens and their changes on propagation in free space,” *JOSA A* **21**, 1907–1916 (2004), DOI [10.1364/JOSAA.21.001907](https://doi.org/10.1364/JOSAA.21.001907). [Primary abstract read](https://opg.optica.org/services/open_md.cfm?uri=josaa-21-10-1907). Its device is explicitly **polarization dependent**, modulates only one component, and has a zero-mean Gaussian random phase. It cannot be substituted for a common scalar random screen when using a factorization theorem.
